% ============================================================
% Introduction
% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:summary-intro}

Modern software systems are composed of many independently compiled components:
libraries, frameworks, microservices, and modules.  When the compiler analyzes
or optimizes a single component, it lacks information about the other components
with which it interacts.  This forces a choice between two extremes:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Whole-program analysis:} Analyze all components together.  This
      provides the most precise results but requires access to all source code
      and is impractical for large, distributed systems.
\item \textbf{Component-local analysis:} Analyze each component in isolation,
      making conservative assumptions about the behavior of other components.
      This is practical but imprecise.
\end{itemize}

\emph{Cross-program semantic summaries} offer a middle ground.  A \emph{summary}
is a compact representation of a component's behavior that can be computed
independently and used by other components' analyses.  Summaries enable
\emph{whole-program analysis without whole-program compilation}: each component
is compiled independently, but the summaries provide enough information for
precise cross-component reasoning.

This approach has deep roots in the interprocedural analysis literature
(\cref{ch:interprocedural}), where function summaries are used for
interprocedural dataflow analysis.  The contribution of this chapter is to
extend the summary concept from \emph{intra-compilation} (function summaries
within a single compilation) to \emph{cross-compilation} (summaries exchanged
between separate compilations).

% ============================================================
% Inter-Procedural Summaries
% ============================================================
\section{Inter-Procedural Summaries}\label{sec:summary-ip}

\subsection{Function Summaries}\label{sec:summary-function}

A \emph{function summary} captures the essential behavior of a function for the
purposes of analysis, abstracting away the implementation details.

\begin{definition}[Function Summary]\label{def:func-summary}
A \emph{function summary} for a function $f$ is a tuple $(\mathit{Pre}_f,
\mathit{Post}_f, \mathit{Side}_f)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathit{Pre}_f$ is the \emph{precondition}: what must hold before calling
      $f$.
\item $\mathit{Post}_f$ is the \emph{postcondition}: what holds after $f$
      returns, given that $\mathit{Pre}_f$ held before.
\item $\mathit{Side}_f$ is the \emph{side-effect summary}: what memory locations
      $f$ may read or write.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Summary Domains}\label{sec:summary-domains}

Different analyses use different summary domains:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Dataflow summaries:} Transfer functions in a dataflow lattice
      (e.g., ``this function kills the available expression $x + 1$'').
\item \textbf{Points-to summaries:} Points-to relations (e.g., ``the return
      value may point to the same object as argument 1'').
\item \textbf{Effect summaries:} Read/write effects (e.g., ``this function may
      write to any location reachable from argument 1'').
\item \textbf{Contract summaries:} Pre/post-conditions in a specification
      language (e.g., ``this function requires $x > 0$ and ensures
      $\mathit{result} = x!$'').
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Summary Composition}\label{sec:summary-composition}

Summaries can be \emph{composed} to obtain the summary of a call sequence:

\begin{definition}[Summary Composition]\label{def:summary-composition}
Given summaries $S_1 = (\mathit{Pre}_1, \mathit{Post}_1, \mathit{Side}_1)$ and
$S_2 = (\mathit{Pre}_2, \mathit{Post}_2, \mathit{Side}_2)$ for functions $f_1$
and $f_2$, the \emph{composed summary} $S_{12}$ for the sequence $f_1; f_2$ is:
\begin{align*}
  \mathit{Pre}_{12} &= \mathit{Pre}_1 \land \mathit{wp}(f_1, \mathit{Pre}_2) \\
  \mathit{Post}_{12} &= \mathit{Post}_2[\mathit{Post}_1 / \text{state}] \\
  \mathit{Side}_{12} &= \mathit{Side}_1 \cup \mathit{Side}_2
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

