% Summary Exchange Between Compilations
% ============================================================
\section{Summary Exchange Between Compilations}\label{sec:summary-exchange}

\subsection{The Summary Exchange Protocol}\label{sec:summary-protocol}

In a multi-component system, each component is compiled independently.  To
enable cross-component analysis, the compiler of component $A$ needs summaries
of the components that $A$ depends on.

\begin{definition}[Summary Exchange]\label{def:summary-exchange}
A \emph{summary exchange} is a protocol by which compiled components publish
their summaries and consume the summaries of their dependencies.  The protocol
specifies:
\begin{itemize}
\item The \emph{summary format}: how summaries are represented.
\item The \emph{summary store}: where summaries are stored and retrieved.
\item The \emph{consistency model}: when summaries are valid and when they must
      be recomputed.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Summary Formats}\label{sec:summary-formats}

Several formats have been proposed for cross-compilation summary exchange:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Module interfaces:} Language-level interface files (e.g., C++
      module interface units, Rust crate metadata) that export type information,
      function signatures, and inline function bodies.
\item \textbf{Analysis summaries:} Compact representations of analysis results
      (e.g., points-to graphs, effect summaries) in a tool-specific format.
\item \textbf{Logical summaries:} Summaries expressed as logical formulae
      (e.g., pre/post-conditions in SMT-LIB format) that can be consumed by
      verification tools.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Summary Granularity}\label{sec:summary-granularity}

The granularity of summaries affects both precision and overhead:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Function-level:} One summary per public function.  Standard for
      most analyses.
\item \textbf{Class-level:} One summary per class or module.  Less precise but
      more compact.
\item \textbf{Path-level:} Summaries for each feasible path through a function.
      More precise but potentially exponential.
\item \textbf{Context-sensitive:} Summaries parameterized by the calling
      context.  Most precise but most expensive.
\end{itemize}

