% Incremental Analysis with Summaries
% ============================================================
\section{Incremental Analysis with Summaries}\label{sec:summary-incremental}

\subsection{Summary Invalidation}\label{sec:summary-invalidate}

When a component changes, its summary must be recomputed.  Moreover, any
component that \emph{depends on} the changed summary must re-analyze.

\begin{definition}[Summary Dependency]\label{def:summary-dep}
Component $B$ \emph{depends on} component $A$'s summary if $B$'s analysis uses
information from $A$'s summary.  When $A$'s summary changes, $B$ must be
re-analyzed.
\end{definition}

The dependency structure forms a DAG.  When a component changes, the set of
components that must be re-analyzed is the set of descendants of the changed
component in the dependency DAG.

\subsection{Incremental Recomputation}\label{sec:summary-incremental-recomp}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Incremental Summary Recomputation}\label{alg:summary-incremental}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Component $C$ changed, dependency DAG $D$, summary store $S$
\Ensure Updated summaries
\State $\mathit{changed} \gets \{C\}$
\State $\mathit{queue} \gets \{C\}$
\While{$\mathit{queue} \neq \emptyset$}
  \State $X \gets \mathit{queue}.\mathit{dequeue}()$
  \State $S[X] \gets \mathit{recompute\_summary}(X)$
  \For{each $Y$ that depends on $X$}
    \If{$Y \notin \mathit{changed}$}
      \State $\mathit{changed} \gets \mathit{changed} \cup \{Y\}$
      \State $\mathit{queue}.\mathit{enqueue}(Y)$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Change Impact Analysis}\label{sec:summary-impact}

\emph{Change impact analysis} determines which summaries are affected by a
change, without recomputing them.  If the change does not affect the summary
(e.g., a change to a private function that does not affect any public
interface), the summary remains valid.

\begin{definition}[Summary-Stable Change]\label{def:stable-change}
A change to component $C$ is \emph{summary-stable} if the summary of $C$ is
unchanged after the change.  Summary-stable changes do not require re-analysis
of dependent components.
\end{definition}

% ============================================================
% Whole-Program Analysis Without Whole-Program Compilation
% ============================================================
\section{Whole-Program Analysis Without Whole-Program Compilation}\label{sec:summary-whole}

\subsection{The Vision}\label{sec:summary-vision}

The goal of cross-program summaries is to enable \emph{whole-program analysis}
(analysis that considers all components together) without requiring
\emph{whole-program compilation} (compiling all components together).  This is
essential for modern software systems where:

\begin{itemize}
\item Components are written in different languages.
\item Components are maintained by different teams.
\item Components are compiled with different compilers or compiler versions.
\item The full source code is not available (e.g., third-party libraries).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Compositional Analysis}\label{sec:summary-compositional}

\emph{Compositional analysis} analyzes each component independently and
combines the results using summary composition.

\begin{theorem}[Compositionality]\label{thm:compositionality}
If the summary domain forms a lattice and the summary composition operation is
monotone, then the compositional analysis computes the same result as the
whole-program analysis.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the fixed-point theorem for monotone functions on complete lattices, the
compositional analysis (which computes the fixed point of the summary
composition) converges to the same result as the whole-program analysis (which
computes the fixed point of the whole-program transfer function), provided that
the summary composition correctly abstracts the whole-program transfer function.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Precision Loss}\label{sec:summary-precision}

In practice, summaries are necessarily \emph{lossy}: they abstract away details
of the function's implementation.  This can lead to \emph{precision loss} in the
compositional analysis.

\begin{example}[Precision Loss]\label{ex:precision-loss}
Consider two functions:
\begin{verbatim}
  int f(int x) { return x > 0 ? x : -x; }
  int g(int x) { return f(x) + f(-x); }
\end{verbatim}
A summary of $f$ might say ``returns a non-negative value.''  Using this
summary, the analysis of $g$ would conclude that $g$ returns a non-negative
value.  But in fact, $g(x) = |x| + |-x| = 2|x|$, which is a stronger property.
The summary loses the correlation between the two calls to $f$.
\end{example}

