\section{Build Systems and Summary Invalidation}\label{sec:summary-build}

\subsection{Build Systems as Summary Managers}\label{sec:summary-build-systems}

Modern build systems (Bazel, Buck, Gradle) already manage a form of summaries:
cached build artifacts.  A build system can be viewed as a summary manager that:
\begin{itemize}
\item Computes summaries (compiled artifacts) for each component.
\item Caches summaries to avoid recomputation.
\item Invalidates summaries when inputs change.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Content-Addressed Summaries}\label{sec:summary-content}

\emph{Content-addressed storage} (used by Nix~\cite{dolstra2004nix} and
Bazel~\cite{bazel2020}) identifies summaries by the hash of their inputs.  This
provides:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Exact invalidation:} A summary is valid if and only if its inputs
      have not changed.
\item \textbf{Sharing:} Identical inputs produce identical summaries, enabling
      sharing across builds.
\item \textbf{Reproducibility:} The same inputs always produce the same summary.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Fine-Grained Invalidation}\label{sec:summary-fine-grained}

Traditional build systems invalidate at the file level: if any source file
changes, all dependent artifacts are rebuilt.  Fine-grained invalidation uses
\emph{dependency tracking} at the level of individual declarations or functions:

\begin{itemize}
\item If function $f$ changes but its type signature does not, only the
      summaries that depend on $f$'s \emph{implementation} (not its interface)
      need to be recomputed.
\item If function $f$ changes its type signature, all summaries that depend on
      $f$'s \emph{interface} must be recomputed.
\end{itemize}

