\section{Applications: Large-Scale Refactoring}\label{sec:summary-refactor}

\subsection{Refactoring as Summary Transformation}\label{sec:summary-refactor-as}

Large-scale refactoring---changing the interface or behavior of a widely-used
component---requires updating all dependent components.  Summaries enable
\emph{automated refactoring} by:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Computing the old summary of the component being refactored.
\item Computing the new summary after the refactoring.
\item Identifying all components whose analysis results depend on the changed
      summary.
\item Automatically updating those components (e.g., renaming call sites,
      adjusting type annotations).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{API Evolution}\label{sec:summary-api}

When a library evolves its API, summaries can track the changes and help
clients migrate:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Deprecation:} The summary marks a function as deprecated, and
      the client's compiler warns at use sites.
\item \textbf{Migration:} The summary includes a mapping from old API to new
      API, enabling automated migration tools.
\item \textbf{Compatibility checking:} The summary records the compatibility
      guarantees (backward-compatible, breaking change), enabling build systems
      to detect incompatible upgrades.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Microservice Analysis}\label{sec:summary-microservice}

In microservice architectures, each service is compiled and deployed
independently.  Cross-service summaries can capture:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{API contracts:} The types and semantics of messages exchanged
      between services.
\item \textbf{Effect summaries:} The side effects of service calls (e.g.,
      database writes, external API calls).
\item \textbf{Performance summaries:} Latency distributions, throughput
      characteristics.
\end{itemize}

These summaries enable whole-system analysis (e.g., detecting API
incompatibilities, predicting cascading failures) without requiring access to
all service implementations.

\begin{insight}
Cross-program semantic summaries represent a shift in how we think about
compilation boundaries.  In the traditional model, the compiler's knowledge
ends at the compilation unit boundary.  With summaries, the compiler can reason
about the entire system, even though it only compiles one component at a time.
This is analogous to the way that type signatures allow modular type checking:
the summary is the ``type'' of the component, capturing everything that other
components need to know.
\end{insight}

\begin{historical}
The idea of using summaries for interprocedural analysis dates to the 1980s,
with the work of Sharir and
Pnueli~\cite{sharir1981interprocedural} on interprocedural dataflow analysis.
The concept of \emph{function summaries} was formalized in the context of
pointer analysis by Callahan et al.~\cite{callahan1986global}.  The extension
to cross-compilation summaries is more recent, driven by the need for scalable
analysis of large, modular software systems.  Modern build systems like
Bazel~\cite{bazel2020} and compilers like Rust~\cite{rust2020} already implement
forms of cross-compilation summaries in their module systems.
\end{historical}

% ============================================================
% Formal Framework
% ============================================================
\section{Formal Framework}\label{sec:summary-formal}

\subsection{Summary Algebras}\label{sec:summary-algebras}

The theory of cross-program summaries can be formalized using \emph{summary
algebras}: algebraic structures that capture the composition and combination of
summaries.

\begin{definition}[Summary Algebra]\label{def:summary-algebra}
A \emph{summary algebra} is a tuple $(\mathcal{S}, \oplus, \otimes, \bottom, \top)$
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(\mathcal{S}, \sqsubseteq)$ is a complete lattice of summaries.
\item $\oplus$ is the \emph{combination} operator (merging summaries from
      different analyses of the same component).
\item $\otimes$ is the \emph{composition} operator (combining summaries of
      sequential components).
\item $\bottom$ is the empty summary (no information).
\item $\top$ is the universal summary (maximum uncertainty).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-Point Convergence]\label{thm:summary-fixedpoint}
If $(\mathcal{S}, \oplus, \otimes)$ is a summary algebra where $\otimes$ is
continuous (preserves directed suprema), then the compositional analysis
converges to the least fixed point of the summary composition operator.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Summary Refinement}\label{sec:summary-refinement}

Summaries can be \emph{refined} iteratively: starting with a coarse summary, the
analysis can request more detail from the component's compiler:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Lazy refinement:} Only refine summaries when the analysis
      determines that more precision is needed.
\item \textbf{On-demand compilation:} Compile additional detail (e.g., inline
      function bodies) only when the summary is insufficient.
\item \textbf{Multi-level summaries:} Summaries at different levels of
      abstraction, from coarse (interface-level) to fine (implementation-level).
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-summary}

Cross-program semantic summaries enable whole-program analysis without
whole-program compilation, bridging the gap between modular compilation and
global reasoning.  By computing compact summaries of each component and
exchanging them between compilations, the compiler can reason about the entire
system while maintaining the practical benefits of separate compilation.

Key open challenges include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Summary precision:} Balancing summary compactness with analysis
      precision.
\item \textbf{Summary format standardization:} Developing standard formats for
      cross-tool summary exchange.
\item \textbf{Incremental maintenance:} Efficiently updating summaries when
      components change.
\item \textbf{Multi-language support:} Exchanging summaries between components
      written in different languages.
\end{itemize}
